\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Shekel slides, politics hurts Slovak crown. \b0\par\par\uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2002\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 11 (Reuters) - The Israeli shekel slid to record lows against the dollar on Monday as the market bet that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem its decline. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown eased on domestic political worries ahead of a no confidence vote in a leading government economic reformers on Wednesday, and worries over possible military moves against Iraq continued to dog the Turkish lira. \par \par \uc2  Dealers reported sales from local mutual funds fuelling the shekel's latest descent, with few players worried that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do anything to cushion its fall. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be concerned about a further depreciation of the shekel, but as long as it isn't reflected in an adjustment of inflation expectations I don't expect them to act," said Ousmene Mandeng, emerging markets analyst at CSFB. \par \par \uc2  Mutual funds have been seen as consistent shekel sellers this year after an aggressive two percent interest rate cut from the Bank of Israel last December substantially reduced their willingness to hold Israeli assets. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the shekel's woes was a fresh escalation of violence in the region. Israel's missiles struck the main security complex in Palestinian-ruled Gaza City on Monday, while its tanks rolled into another Palestinian enclave at Nablus. \par \par \uc2  The moves were a retaliation after two Palestinians sprayed gunfire at a cafe near an Israeli army base in southern Israel on Sunday, killing two women soldiers before being shot dead themselves. \par \par \uc2  Analysts often play down the influence of the Israeli-Palestinian conflict on shekel moves, but Mandeng at CSFB said that it was hampering the recovery of the local economy. \par \par \uc2  "We've had no progress on the security front and that is affecting the real economy. As long as these factors do not change, it's difficult to see the upside in the shekel," he said. \par \par \uc2  At 1115 GMT, the shekel traded at 4.7370 to the dollar from 4.6610 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  POLITICS WEIGHS ON SLOVAK CROWN \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown slipped on political worries ahead of a parliamentary no-confidence motion on Wednesday against the reformist deputy premier for the economy Ivan Miklos, moved by opposition deputies. \par \par \uc2  If Miklos were to go, it would be seen as a blow to the country's aspirations to join the EU in the first enlargement wave. \par \par \uc2  However, a senior official in the coalition-member leftist SDL party said on Friday that he did not expect his party to join the opposition move. \par \par \uc2  The SDL was instrumental in forcing the resignation of another leading reformer, the former finance minister Brigita Schmognerova, last month. \par \par \uc2  "The movements in the crown have been pretty mild as it it isn't surprising news. This isn't the first time that Miklos has faced calls for his head," said Jeff Gable, emerging markets analyst at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  However, Gable noted that the ruling coalition parties are tentatively scheduled to meet on Tuesday to discuss the SDL's proposals to alter the sale of the gas monopoly SPP. \par \par \uc2  "Its unlikely that the SDL's demands will be met and that may darken their mood somewhat. That is the risk," said Gable. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown traded at 42.50 to the euro from 42.35 and at 48.47 to the dollar from 48.45. \par \par \uc2  The Turkish lira fell briefly below 1.4 million to the dollar early on Monday with nerves over possible military action against Iraq still dogging sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Turkish president Bulent Ecevit said on Friday that Iraqi president Saddam Hussain had indicated no change of position on allowing U.N. arms inspection teams into Iraq, in response to an appeal from Turkey last week. \par \par \uc2  However, the local press released what it claimed were the contents of Saddam's reply over the weekend, containing none of the aggressive rhetoric rumoured in the market last week. \par \par \uc2  The lira traded at 1,385,000 to the dollar on the interbank market from 1,376,000 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020211dy2b00mjh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
