\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if SKK easing continues. \b0\par\par\uc2 230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2000\par 12:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Dec 4 (Reuters) - The director of the Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (NBS) treasury department, Frantisek Szulenyi, said on Monday that the bank could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from easing if its recent trend continued. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown was bid at 43.51 to its key reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, at 1120 GMT, from 43.21/26 late on Friday and 43.24/32 at the open. The Slovak unit has eased steadily from bid at around 42.5 to the euro on November 23. \par \par \uc2  "Of course we are watching the market. We are not quite happy about the development in the market, but so far we don't think there is a need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Szulenyi told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "Of course if the development continues as it has in the last couple of days, definitely we will step into the market, but we still think at this level it wouldn't be very appropriate to step in." \par \par \uc2  Szulenyi added commercial banks said the easing was supported by client orders and could also be caused by the dip in the rate of the U.S. dollar against the euro. The crown was trading at 48.97/07 to the dollar, from 49.50/58 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown often reacts conversely with euro movements against the dollar and vice-versa. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwc40bb9l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
