\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Slovak Deputy PM says economic stability reached. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Javurek \par \uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2000\par 14:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, July 27 (Reuters) - Deputy Prime Minister for the Economy Ivan Miklos said on Thursday that Slovakia had reached economic stability and that he expected inflation to fall below 10 percent soon. \par \par \uc2  He said continued reforms could lead to Slovakia's credit ratings to be upgraded to investment grade in the first half of next year. \par \par \uc2  "All the economic indicators are showing that we have managed to reach macroeconomic stabilisation after a year since we introduced a package of measures aiming to that effect," Miklos told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "Inflation will soon be under the level of 10 percent, and a further downward trend will continue in the first months of next year." \par \par \uc2  Slovak consumer prices fell 0.1 percent in June from May, taking year-on-year inflation to 15.4 percent. Core inflation, which excluded the effect of regulated prices and tax changes, came in at 5.8 percent year-on-year in June. \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rate of the crown can be seen at present as stabilised," Miklos said. \par \par \uc2  "The (crown's) rate since October 1998 has decreased only against the American dollar, but this movement is caused by a significant strengthening of the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the euro over the last year." \par \par \uc2  Earlier this year the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced for the first time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent its unwanted firming. \par \par \uc2  Miklos said that he expected the country's corporate sphere to revive, boosted by a significant decrease in Slovak interbank market rates and subsequently also in commercial loan rates. \par \par \uc2  "While the average loan interest rate was at 23.5 percent in October 1998, in June 2000 it was only 10.8 percent," Miklos said in a prepared statement distributed at the conference. \par \par \uc2  "We expect lower financial costs (for loans) to help the corporate sphere to revive," he said. "This should, with some delay, increase also the state budget income from tax payments." \par \par \uc2  Miklos also said Slovakia's ratings with international agencies could return to investment grade, from where they fell in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "In continuing reforms, we can expect improving of ratings and moving Slovakia into the investment range again in the first half of 2001," the statement said. \par \par \uc2  Miklos said, however, that next year would be "quite difficult" in fiscal terms. \par \par \uc2  Among other factors, the cancellation of an import surcharge, currently at three percent, and lower individual income taxes and a 10 percent increase in pensions will cause fiscal pressure next year. ((Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-5341-8402, fax 421-7-5341-8403, bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw7r09y4i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
