\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank says no need to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on crown. \b0\par\par\uc2 175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2000\par 13:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, June 16 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, Frantisek Szulenyi, said on Friday he saw no need for the bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market despite a continued weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I don't see any reason for us to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Szulenyi told Reuters. The crown was quoted at 42.264/364 at 1122 GMT on Friday after opening quotes of around 42.07/15. Dealers say the weakening reflected continued bearish sentiment following Thursday's release of worse than expected first quarter gross domestic product figures. \par \par \uc2  GDP growth in the first quarter came in at 1.5 percent, well below market expectations of a 2.78 percent year-on-year rise. \par \par \uc2  The crown was trading at 41.900/980 to the euro just after the GDP figures were released compared with 41.850/920 at Wednesday's close. ((Robin Shepherd, Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-5341-8402, fax 421-7-5341-8403, bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6g03hzp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
