\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Slovak CPI beats expectations, boosts crown. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 10:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, May 12 (Reuters) - Slovakia's headline inflation rate for April came in below market expectations on Friday giving relief to the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which edged up to 42.8 against the euro after 43.15 at the open. \par \par \uc2  The statistical office said April inflation was 0.0 percent on a month-on-month basis and 15.9 percent year-on-year. A Reuters poll of analysts had predicted an average monthly inflation figure of 0.3 percent and 16.4 percent on an annualised basis. \par \par \uc2  "It was mainly foreign banks who were buying crowns since the market opened but this trend was visibly boosted by favourable economic indicators," Marek Senkovic a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Istrobank said. \par \par \uc2  The crown has weakened in the last week prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the fall. The decline began at around 41.7 to the euro on May 4, driven, analysts say, by fears over the stability of the coalition government. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the depreciation was not based on economic fundamentals, and its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were designed to counter volatility. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice Governor Elena Kohutikova said on Thursday she believed headline inflation had peaked at current levels and that it would decline to reach 10 percent by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The statistical office also said on Friday that core inflation - headline inflation stripped of the effects of regulated price and indirect tax changes - was minus 0.1 percent month-on-month compared with a 0.3 percent rise in March and 6.4 percent year-on-year after 7.0 percent in March. \par \par \uc2  Industrial production was 8.5 percent year-on-year in March compared with a downwardly revised 8.0 percent increase in February and construction output fell 16.1 percent year-on-year after an 11.7 percent decline in February. ((Robin Shepherd, Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-5341-8402, fax 421-7-5341-8403, bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5c00kyo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
