\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - IMF urges Slovakia to cut spending, curb  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 15:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In BRATISLAVA story 'IMF urges Slovakia to cut spending, curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' \par \par \uc2  please read in fifth paragraph '...with GDP itself seen growing at 2.5 percent.' \par \par \uc2  This corrects GDP figure. \par \par \uc2  (The Slovak finance ministry, which issued the report, corrected the figure which, \par \par \uc2  due to a software problem, was improperly transcribed from the IMF). \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows. \par \par \uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, May 12 (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) released a report on the strengths and weaknesses of the Slovak economy on Monday, highlighting recommendations to avoid a big appreciation in the crown and curb public spending. \par \par \uc2  The IMF report comes after several days of depreciation pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and two rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem volatility. \par \par \uc2  The report praised the government's privatisation programme and also plans to sterilise foreign direct investment inflows through special accounts at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "...A sustained and significant appreciation of the exchange rate would not be desirable at this juncture as it could adversely affect external competitiveness and result in a widening of the external current account deficit," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF added it expected the current account deficit to be around 5.0 percent of GDP in 2000 with GDP itself seen growing at 2.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's main concerns centred on public spending, particularly increased expenditure financed by privatisation proceeds. \par \par \uc2  "The mission is concerned about plans to increase government expenditure, contingent on financing from privatisation proceeds." \par \par \uc2  "It now appears that the deficit of the general government - excluding planned additional expenditure associated with privatisation proceeds - could approach four percent of GDP in 2000." \par \par \uc2  The report added that this figure could rise to around five percent unless such planned expenditures were reversed or offsetting spending measures were adopted. \par \par \uc2  Privatisation proceeds would be well used, the IMF said, if they were employed to retire foreign debt and postpone the need for further foreign debt issuance. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said that a public sector deficit of closer to three percent of GDP would be preferable because it would help build credibility among potential foreign investors and it would relieve spending pressures future years. \par \par \uc2  However, a rise in the fiscal deficit of one percent of GDP would be acceptable if funds were used to effect banking system reform which the IMF said was a crucial aspect of overall structural reform. \par \par \uc2  The mission was postive on Slovakia's inflation prospects. \par \par \uc2  "The mission does not see any significant inflationary pressures ahead, as long as wage settlements are moderate and the public finances remain under control." \par \par \uc2  "In these circumstances, there may be room for further, moderate interest rate cuts." \par \par \uc2  Unemployment had peaked at around the current level - 19.3 percent in March - but it was likely to remain high. ((Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-5341-8402, fax 421-7-5341-8403, bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5c00hxm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscal