\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Nasdaq takes shine off zloty. \b0\par\par\uc2 797 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2000\par 12:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 11 (Reuters) - East European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly firm on Tuesday in thin trade apart from the Polish zloty which slid in response to a drop in Wall Street's tech-heavy Nasdaq index overnight. \par \par \uc2  Poland, which has the largest concentration of technology, media and telecom stocks of any East European bourse, and is also the largest regional market, saw the zloty slide to 5.29 percent above parity against its basket of euros and dollars from close to six percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The capital account has shown a lot more portfolio flows, it is just a sentiment thing, people feel the zloty is more exposed globally," said Tim Ash, analyst at West LB. \par \par \uc2  The zloty was trading at 3.9925 bid to the euro by 0958 GMT, compared with 3.9965 at the open and 3.9769 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  Ash believes zloty weakness should be used by investors as an entry point. \par \par \uc2  "You are still getting 600-700 basis points for Polish paper over Hungary," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Hungarian forint was quiet on Tuesday, with more positive news on the economy outweighing a reports in a local newspaper that National Bank of Hungary Gyorgy Suranyi had tendered his resignation which had been turned down by the government. \par \par \uc2  Although Suranyi's departure is a perennial in the markets, analysts and traders said it would certainly be received badly, given Hungary's sometimes fractious government coalition. \par \par \uc2  "If he did go, it would be important. He has often been the last man standing in terms of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime," said West LB's Ash. \par \par \uc2  The forint was trading at 258.29 bid compared with 258.10 on Monday, with investors continuing to fall out of love with East Europe's most succesful economy. \par \par \uc2  Data on Tuesday showed Hungary's March headline consumer prices rose 9.6 percent rise year-on-year and one percent month-on-month. \par \par \uc2  Core inflation was 8.6 percent year-on-year and 0.4 percent month-on-month. \par \par \uc2  In the Czech Republic, the release of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary meeting notes opened the possibility of another interest rate cut, with comments that the continuation of the strengthening of the crown "could lead to an unwanted tightening of monetary conditions". \par \par \uc2  The Czech crown was trading at 36.438 bid to the euro moderately lower than 36.21 bid on Monday. \par \par \uc2  In Slovakia, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to keep investors on their toes, with comments that it was "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  discreetly" in the market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown was at 41.526 bid to the euro, little changed from 41.525 on Monday. ((David Chance, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6784 email david.chance@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2   ** Following is a table of key emerging market exchange rates, \par \par \uc2  including the change versus late New York trade the previous day \par \par \uc2  and the change in the year to date. \par \par \uc2   ** The Czech, Polish, Hungarian and Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , formerly \par \par \uc2  quoted against the mark, are now quoted against the euro to \par \par \uc2  reflect new market norms. All other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are quoted \par \par \uc2  against the dollar. \par \par \uc2   ** According to market convention, exchange rates are quoted in \par \par \uc2  units per dollar or euro and the percentage change denotes the \par \par \uc2  rise or fall of the dollar or euro value. \par \par \uc2   ** Annual percentage changes are calculated from late on the \par \par \uc2  first day of the current trading year, January 3. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab \tab     LATEST  PREV    PCT CHG  PCT CHG\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   CLOSE\tab \tab in 2000\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPE/MEAST/AFRICA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Czech Rep.\tab \tab     36.49   36.24   0.68%   1.12%\par Poland\tab \tab \tab   3.99    3.98    0.36%   -4.74%\par Hungary\tab \tab \tab  258.3   257.96  0.13%   1.54%\par Slovakia\tab \tab \tab 41.52   41.45   0.17%   -1.76%\par Romania\tab \tab \tab  19,635  19,610  0.13%   8.72%\par Ukraine\tab \tab \tab  5.50    5.52    -0.36%  5.77%\par South Africa\tab \tab   6.59    6.56    0.53%   7.54%\par Kenya\tab \tab \tab    74.37   74.70   -0.44%  2.23%\par Israel\tab \tab \tab   4.034   4.019   0.36%   -1.69%\par Turkey\tab \tab \tab   589,500 589,830 -0.06%  9.38%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LATAM\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mexico\tab \tab \tab   9.34    9.35    -0.14%  -0.69%\par Brazil\tab \tab \tab   1.7420  1.7420  0.00%   -4.23%\par Venezuela\tab \tab      670.75  670.75  0.00%   3.39%\par Chile\tab \tab \tab    504.90  504.90  0.00%   -4.32%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore\tab \tab      1.720   1.717   0.13%   3.84%\par Hong Kong\tab \tab      7.786   7.787   -0.01%  0.13%\par China\tab \tab \tab    8.279   8.280   -0.01%  -0.01%\par Indonesia\tab \tab      7,600   7,615   -0.20%  8.11%\par Thailand\tab \tab \tab 38.06   38.01   0.13%   2.89%\par Philippines\tab \tab    40.95   41.05   -0.24%  2.63%\par Taiwan\tab \tab \tab   30.29   30.25   0.12%   -3.24%\par South Korea\tab \tab    1,110   1,110   0.00%   -1.55%\par India\tab \tab \tab    43.63   43.56   0.15%   0.48%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\pa