\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to weaken crown-dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2 154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2000\par 09:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Feb 1 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reappeared on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown at levels between 42.29 and 42.3 to the euro, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Finally the National Bank is on the market again," said one dealer who refused to be named. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown at these levels on several occasions since December last year. \par \par \uc2  Governor Marian Jusko has said the bank does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter trends but that it has a secret preferred range, which can move along with changes to fundamentals, within which it wants to see the crown. \par \par \uc2  In a recent interview with Reuters, however, he said economic fundamentals themselves were affected by the rate of the crown. \par \par \uc2  ((Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-5341-8402, fax 421-7-5341-8403, bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dw21036t0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
