\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank says would  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to cap crown. \b0\par\par\uc2 193 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2000\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Jan 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  asset department head Frantisek Szulenyi said on Monday the bank would take action if the crown firmed strongly over 42.300 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  "Should it go significantly over the 300 level we would do something," Szulenyi told Reuters. When asked to clarify whether that specifically meant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  he said: "Yes, probably we would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ..." \par \par \uc2  The crown was quoted at 42.260/340 to the euro at 1121 GMT. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Marian Jusko said on December 22 the bank would aim to keep the crown at around prevailing levels - 42.3 to the euro - in 2000, but acknowledged there would probably be pressure for an appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The government is anxious to avoid a sharp strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it fears could hit the country's export-led growth at a time of flagging domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in four sessions in December to cap crown strength at 42.3 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  ((Bratislava Newsroom, 421-7-5341-8402, fax, 421-7-5341-8403 bratislava.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dw1301gbl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
